    "['local-address [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'neighbor 172.17.1.1 update-source 172.17.1.2', 'parent_command': ['router bgp [parameter1]', 'neighbor [parameter1] remote-as [parameter2]'], 'root': 'router bgp [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nneighbor 172.17.1.1 update-source 172.17.1.2\\n##\\n\\nExplanation: \\n- In Juniper, `local-address` specifies the source IP for BGP sessions\\n- In Cisco, the equivalent is `update-source` under the BGP neighbor configuration\\n- The command tells BGP to use 172.17.1.2 as the source IP when communicating with neighbor 172.17.1.1', 'trans_template': 'neighbor 172.17.1.1 update-source 172.17.1.2'}]]": 1,
    "['neighbor [parameter2] peer-as [parameter3]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'router bgp [local-as-number]', 'parent_command': ['router bgp [parameter1]', 'neighbor [parameter1] remote-as [parameter2]'], 'root': 'router bgp [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper BGP peer AS configuration:\\n\\n##\\nrouter bgp [local-as-number]\\n neighbor 172.17.1.1 remote-as 100\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Cisco IOS, you need to first specify your local AS number in the `router bgp` context (replace [local-as-number] with your actual local AS number), then configure the peer's AS number using the `neighbor remote-as` command. The Juniper configuration doesn't show the local AS number, so it's represented as a placeholder here.\", 'trans_template': 'router bgp [local-as-number]'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'neighbor 172.17.1.1 remote-as 100', 'parent_command': ['router bgp [parameter1]', 'neighbor [parameter1] remote-as [parameter2]'], 'root': 'router bgp [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper BGP peer AS configuration:\\n\\n##\\nrouter bgp [local-as-number]\\n neighbor 172.17.1.1 remote-as 100\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Cisco IOS, you need to first specify your local AS number in the `router bgp` context (replace [local-as-number] with your actual local AS number), then configure the peer's AS number using the `neighbor remote-as` command. The Juniper configuration doesn't show the local AS number, so it's represented as a placeholder here.\", 'trans_template': 'neighbor 172.17.1.1 remote-as 100'}]]": 1,
    "['family inet [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [0, 0], 'trans_command': 'address-family [parameter1]', 'parent_command': ['router bgp [parameter1]'], 'root': 'router bgp [parameter1]'}, {'para_map': [0, 0], 'trans_command': 'address-family [parameter1] [parameter2]', 'parent_command': ['router bgp [parameter1]'], 'root': 'router bgp [parameter1]'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'exit-address-family', 'parent_command': ['router bgp [parameter1]'], 'root': 'router bgp [parameter1]'}]]": 1,
    "['neighbor [neighbor_ip] import [import_policy]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'router bgp 65000', 'parent_command': ['router bgp [parameter1]', 'neighbor [parameter1] remote-as [parameter2]'], 'root': 'router bgp [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translation of the Juniper command `set protocols bgp group external neighbor 172.17.1.1 import IMPORT-DIRECT` to Cisco IOS:\\n\\n##\\nrouter bgp 65000\\n neighbor 172.17.1.1 route-map IMPORT-DIRECT in\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. I assumed BGP AS 65000 for the Cisco router since the original Juniper configuration doesn\\'t specify the local AS number.\\n2. The Cisco equivalent uses a route-map instead of a policy-statement.\\n3. The route-map \"IMPORT-DIRECT\" would need to be separately configured to match directly connected routes, similar to the Juniper policy.', 'trans_template': 'router bgp 65000'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'neighbor 172.17.1.1 route-map IMPORT-DIRECT in', 'parent_command': ['router bgp [parameter1]', 'neighbor [parameter1] remote-as [parameter2]'], 'root': 'router bgp [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translation of the Juniper command `set protocols bgp group external neighbor 172.17.1.1 import IMPORT-DIRECT` to Cisco IOS:\\n\\n##\\nrouter bgp 65000\\n neighbor 172.17.1.1 route-map IMPORT-DIRECT in\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. I assumed BGP AS 65000 for the Cisco router since the original Juniper configuration doesn\\'t specify the local AS number.\\n2. The Cisco equivalent uses a route-map instead of a policy-statement.\\n3. The route-map \"IMPORT-DIRECT\" would need to be separately configured to match directly connected routes, similar to the Juniper policy.', 'trans_template': 'neighbor 172.17.1.1 route-map IMPORT-DIRECT in'}]]": 1,
    "['set policy-options policy-statement [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'router bgp 65000', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'router bgp 65000', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translation of the Juniper command `set protocols bgp group external neighbor 172.17.1.1 import IMPORT-DIRECT` to Cisco IOS:\\n\\n##\\nrouter bgp 65000\\n neighbor 172.17.1.1 route-map IMPORT-DIRECT in\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. I assumed a local AS number of 65000 since it wasn't specified in the Juniper configuration (Cisco requires the local AS to be specified in the router bgp context)\\n2. The route-map `IMPORT-DIRECT` would need to be separately configured to match directly connected routes, similar to the Juniper policy-statement\", 'trans_template': 'router bgp 65000'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'neighbor 172.17.1.1 route-map IMPORT-DIRECT in', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'neighbor 172.17.1.1 route-map IMPORT-DIRECT in', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translation of the Juniper command `set protocols bgp group external neighbor 172.17.1.1 import IMPORT-DIRECT` to Cisco IOS:\\n\\n##\\nrouter bgp 65000\\n neighbor 172.17.1.1 route-map IMPORT-DIRECT in\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. I assumed a local AS number of 65000 since it wasn't specified in the Juniper configuration (Cisco requires the local AS to be specified in the router bgp context)\\n2. The route-map `IMPORT-DIRECT` would need to be separately configured to match directly connected routes, similar to the Juniper policy-statement\", 'trans_template': 'neighbor 172.17.1.1 route-map IMPORT-DIRECT in'}]]": 1,
    "['term [parameter2] from protocol [parameter3]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'route-map IMPORT-DIRECT permit 10', 'parent_command': ['router bgp 65000'], 'root': 'router bgp 65000', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper configuration:\\n\\n##\\nroute-map IMPORT-DIRECT permit 10\\n match protocol connected\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- Juniper's `policy-statement` translates to Cisco's `route-map`\\n- The `term 1` becomes the sequence number `10` (Cisco typically uses increments of 10)\\n- `from protocol direct` in Juniper matches directly connected routes, which is `match protocol connected` in Cisco\\n- The implicit `then accept` in Juniper becomes the `permit` action in Cisco's route-map\", 'trans_template': 'route-map IMPORT-DIRECT permit 10'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'match protocol connected', 'parent_command': ['router bgp 65000'], 'root': 'router bgp 65000', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper configuration:\\n\\n##\\nroute-map IMPORT-DIRECT permit 10\\n match protocol connected\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- Juniper's `policy-statement` translates to Cisco's `route-map`\\n- The `term 1` becomes the sequence number `10` (Cisco typically uses increments of 10)\\n- `from protocol direct` in Juniper matches directly connected routes, which is `match protocol connected` in Cisco\\n- The implicit `then accept` in Juniper becomes the `permit` action in Cisco's route-map\", 'trans_template': 'match protocol connected'}]]": 1,
